# <%= title %>

A Nuxeo Satori business app: an app definition package
([package-pipeline.md](<%= docs %>/package-pipeline.md), section 1). The `app-package` generator
scaffolded it with the files below; they are yours to change.

| File                                                 | What it is                                                     |
| ---------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `bundle/agentic-ui-config/app.json`                  | The app: its id, name, version, platform range and groups      |
| `bundle/agentic-ui-config/labels/en.json`            | Its labels, in English                                         |
<% if (type) { %>| `bundle/agentic-ui-config/layouts/<%= type %>/view.layout.json` | The record page of a <%= type %> |
<% } %>| `bundle/OSGI-INF/<%= name %>-config.xml`             | The component, which contributes the app<% if (type) { %> and its record page<% } %> to the server |
| `bundle/META-INF/MANIFEST.MF`                        | The bundle's manifest, which names the component               |
| `package/package.xml`                                | The Marketplace package: name, version, what it depends on     |
| `package/install.xml`                                | What installing it does: it copies the bundle, and nothing else |
| `build.mjs`                                          | Version 1's builder: the generator's, byte for byte            |

What each file may say is [app-definition-v2.md](<%= docs %>/app-definition-v2.md) and
[layout-v2.md](<%= docs %>/layout-v2.md).

## Adding to it

Each of these generators writes its file and has the component name it. From the repository's
root:

```bash
# A record page, and an Edit form
npx nx g ./tools/satori-generators:app-layout --package=<%= projectRoot %> --type=<Type> --model=<content-model.json>
npx nx g ./tools/satori-generators:app-form --package=<%= projectRoot %> --type=<Type> --mode=edit --model=<content-model.json>
# A queue, queues/<id>.json
npx nx g ./tools/satori-generators:app-queue --package=<%= projectRoot %> --id=<queue> --pageProvider=<page provider>
# A button, in actions.json
npx nx g ./tools/satori-generators:app-action --package=<%= projectRoot %> --id=<%= appId %>.<name> --transition=<transition>
```

## Languages

`labels/en.json` is the app's English, and the only labels file written here. Every other language
comes from Crowdin: the repository's `crowdin-conf.yml` reads each kit's `labels/en.json` as a
source and writes its approved translations beside it, as `labels/<language>.json`. Do not write
one by hand; the next pull from Crowdin replaces it. Adding a language on Crowdin's side is the
administrator's guide's, [section 4, "Languages"](<%= docs %>/administrator-guide.md#languages).

- **A language gives a subset of English's keys.** Each key it leaves out shows in English, key by
  key: the app registry serves the file over `labels/en.json`, and the app falls back to English
  besides. The pipeline refuses a key that `labels/en.json` has no text for.
- **When Crowdin's pull request brings this kit's first file in a language,** name the file in the
  component and raise the version, a minor at least, in the same pull request. Until then the
  pipeline refuses the package, since the registry would never serve a file it does not name:

  ```bash
  npx nx g ./tools/satori-generators:app-registry --package=<%= projectRoot %>
  ```

- **A label whose meaning changes gets a new key.** Crowdin keeps the translations of a key whose
  English text changes, so a key reused for something else keeps translations of the old meaning.
- **`npm run i18n:coverage`** says how much of each language this kit holds, and which keys each
  is missing.

## Checking and building it

```bash
npm run app-package -- <%= projectRoot %> --platform <platform version>
```

It writes `dist/<%= name %>-<version>.zip`. `--model <file>` holds the layouts to a server's
content model; `npm run app-package:model` exports one. A version is raised in three places,
together: `package/package.xml`, `app.json`'s `packageVersion`, and the manifest's
`Bundle-Version`.
